Below is a short “road‑map” that shows which tables / columns are needed, the main filtering conditions, and ten exploratory BigQuery statements that gradually lead from simple inspection of the data to the full band‑level summary that you finally need.  
(The last query already delivers the requested numbers and frequencies, but every previous query is kept for step‑by‑step validation.)

Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• `Cytogen.RefNo`, `Cytogen.CaseNo` – primary keys that identify a patient case.  
• `Cytogen.Morph` – morphology code; use `='3111'` for breast carcinoma.  
• `Cytogen.Topo`  – topography code; use `='0401'` for adenocarcinoma.  
• `CytoConverted.RefNo`, `CytoConverted.CaseNo`, `CytoConverted.InvNo`, `CytoConverted.Clone` – link each segment to the cytogenetic case.  
• `CytoConverted.Chr`, `CytoConverted.Start`, `CytoConverted.End` – genomic coordinates of the segment.  
• `CytoConverted.Type` – copy‑number class (“Gain”, “Loss”, often also “Amplification”, “HomLoss”, etc. – verify first).  
• `CytoBands_hg38.chromosome`, `CytoBands_hg38.hg38_start`, `CytoBands_hg38.hg38_stop`, `CytoBands_hg38.cytoband_name` – definition of each cytogenetic band together with its order on the genome (implicit in `hg38_start`).  

Main conditions  
• Breast cancer cohort: `Cytogen.Morph = '3111'`.  
• Adenocarcinoma cohort: `Cytogen.Topo  = '0401'`.  
• Keep only CytoConverted rows that belong to either of the two cohorts (inner join on `RefNo` + `CaseNo`).  
• Join each CytoConverted segment to the cytoband that fully contains its [`Start`,`End`] interval (simple condition: band.hg38_start ≤ segment.Start AND band.hg38_stop ≥ segment.End).  
• Group by `cytoband_name` and by copy‑number `Type`; count absolute numbers, then divide by the number of distinct cases to obtain the percentage (two decimals).  
• Sorting: 1) chromosome ordinal (take numeric part of `cytoBands_hg38.chromosome`), 2) `hg38_start`.  

Ten BigQuery SQL snippets
────────────────────────

```sql
-- 1. List a few morphology codes to check that '3111' really corresponds to breast carcinoma
SELECT DISTINCT `Morph`
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. List a few topography codes to confirm that '0401' is adenocarcinoma
SELECT DISTINCT `Topo`
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Preview CytoConverted “Type” values (Gain, Loss, Amplification, etc.)
SELECT DISTINCT `Type`
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Count how many CytoConverted rows belong to breast‑cancer cases
SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_segments
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` cc
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen` cy
  ON cc.RefNo  = cy.RefNo
 AND cc.CaseNo = cy.CaseNo
WHERE cy.Morph = '3111';
```

```sql
-- 5. Same, but for adenocarcinoma (topography = ‘0401’)
SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_segments
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` cc
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen` cy
  ON cc.RefNo  = cy.RefNo
 AND cc.CaseNo = cy.CaseNo
WHERE cy.Topo = '0401';
```

```sql
-- 6. How many distinct patient cases are in each cohort?
SELECT
  COUNT(DISTINCT CONCAT(RefNo,'-',CaseNo)) AS n_cases,
  'breast' AS cohort
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
WHERE Morph = '3111'
UNION ALL
SELECT
  COUNT(DISTINCT CONCAT(RefNo,'-',CaseNo)) AS n_cases,
  'adenocarcinoma' AS cohort
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
WHERE Topo = '0401';
```

```sql
-- 7. Join one sample segment to the correct cytoband (sanity check of the interval join)
SELECT
  cc.RefNo, cc.CaseNo, cc.Chr, cc.Start, cc.End,
  b.cytoband_name
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` cc
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen` cy
  ON cy.RefNo  = cc.RefNo
 AND cy.CaseNo = cc.CaseNo
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.CytoBands_hg38` b
  ON b.chromosome = cc.Chr
 AND b.hg38_start <= cc.Start
 AND b.hg38_stop  >= cc.End
WHERE cy.Morph = '3111'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Band‑level counts for the breast‑cancer cohort only
SELECT
  b.cytoband_name,
  cc.Type,
  COUNT(*) AS n_events
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` cc
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen` cy
  ON cy.RefNo  = cc.RefNo
 AND cy.CaseNo = cc.CaseNo
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.CytoBands_hg38` b
  ON b.chromosome = cc.Chr
 AND b.hg38_start <= cc.Start
 AND b.hg38_stop  >= cc.End
WHERE cy.Morph = '3111'
GROUP BY b.cytoband_name, cc.Type
ORDER BY b.cytoband_name, cc.Type
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Same, but adenocarcinoma cohort; include number of cases to compute %
WITH cases AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT CONCAT(RefNo,'-',CaseNo) AS case_id
  FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
  WHERE Topo = '0401'
)
SELECT
  b.cytoband_name,
  cc.Type,
  COUNT(*) AS n_events,
  ROUND(100*COUNT(*)/ (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM cases), 2) AS freq_pct
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` cc
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen` cy
  ON cy.RefNo  = cc.RefNo
 AND cy.CaseNo = cc.CaseNo
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.CytoBands_hg38` b
  ON b.chromosome = cc.Chr
 AND b.hg38_start <= cc.Start
 AND b.hg38_stop  >= cc.End
WHERE cy.Topo = '0401'
GROUP BY b.cytoband_name, cc.Type
ORDER BY b.cytoband_name, cc.Type
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL QUERY – events and frequencies for BOTH cohorts, requested sort order
WITH cohort_cases AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT CONCAT(RefNo,'-',CaseNo) AS case_id
  FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
  WHERE Morph = '3111' OR Topo = '0401'
),
band_events AS (
  SELECT
    b.chromosome,
    b.hg38_start,
    b.hg38_stop,
    b.cytoband_name,
    cc.Type,
    COUNT(*) AS n_events
  FROM `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` cc
  JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen` cy
    ON cy.RefNo  = cc.RefNo
   AND cy.CaseNo = cc.CaseNo
  JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.CytoBands_hg38` b
    ON b.chromosome = cc.Chr
   AND b.hg38_start <= cc.Start
   AND b.hg38_stop  >= cc.End
  WHERE cy.Morph = '3111'        -- breast carcinoma
     OR cy.Topo  = '0401'        -- adenocarcinoma
  GROUP BY b.chromosome, b.hg38_start, b.hg38_stop,
           b.cytoband_name, cc.Type
)
SELECT
  cytoband_name,
  Type                 AS copy_number_class,      -- Amplification / Gain / Loss / HomLoss
  n_events             AS events,
  ROUND(100 * n_events / (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM cohort_cases), 2) AS freq_pct
FROM band_events
ORDER BY
  SAFE_CAST(REGEXP_REPLACE(chromosome, r'chr', '') AS INT64),   -- chromosome order
  hg38_start,
  Type;
```

Each query returns at most 20 rows (except the final one, which you may run without LIMIT to get the full result).
